        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Use it to learn in ways that fit them",
            "content": "He says his children use ChatGPT to quiz themselves before tests, in audio or chat mode, and describes it as better than cue cards. His daughter also photographed a page of Shakespeare she didn’t understand and asked ChatGPT to explain it. More broadly, he sees it as a personalized tutor that can teach visually, through audio, or through reading.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Build the habit of turning to it for questions",
            "content": "When his oldest daughter was learning to code and making a website, Bret would have her use ChatGPT whenever she had a question for him. He says he wants his children to learn to use the tool constructively in their lives, and encourages them to make AI part of their lives.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S2",
              "S3"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Treat it as a learning utility, with a caveat",
            "content": "Bret thinks of ChatGPT more like Google Search—a utility for learning—than like a phone, which he says he’d wait a long time to give a child. He also cautions that these tools can help students avoid learning if that is their aim, so parents and teachers have a role in encouraging constructive use.",
